+# Zero-byte thumbnails in staging — OPEN
+
+Staging produced zero-byte objects in the CDN bucket at roughly 1 upload in 40.
+A `time.sleep(2)` in `render.py` made the symptom stop. The cause was never
+established. **Treat this as open, not fixed.** The sections below separate what
+is established from what is guessed, so neither gets re-argued.
+
+## Established
+
+Each of these was checked against the code or measured. They are not opinions.
+
+**1. The sleep cannot be fixing a cache-read race.** `render.py:44-46` reads and
+decodes the source, *then* sleeps, *then* uploads. The read has already
+completed when the delay begins. Position alone rules out every hypothesis that
+explains the fix via the cache file. The delay only postpones the network call.
+
+**2. A torn or empty cache read cannot produce a zero-byte thumbnail.** Measured
+against PIL: empty input raises `UnidentifiedImageError`; input truncated to
+10%, 50% and 90% raises `OSError: image file is truncated`. A successful decode
+always emits a header, so `thumbnail()` never returns `b""`. `worker.py:33` runs
+`render.py` with `check=True`, so any of these surfaces as a crash, not as a
+silent empty object.
+
+**3. `worker.py` does hold a lock. `render.py` does not.** This corrects the
+handover, which had it as "both read the same way". `worker.py:12-22`
+(`source_is_complete`) acquires `LOCK_SH`, which blocks until the upload
+handler releases its exclusive write lock, and then requires nonzero size.
+Measured, not inferred: against a writer holding `LOCK_EX` for 1.0s over a
+file that starts zero-length, the gate blocks for the remaining 0.80s and
+returns true only once the payload is on disk. The gate is sound. `worker.py`
+"never failed" because there was nothing there to fail. `render.py:15` reads
+with no lock at all.
+
+> Consequence worth knowing: `render.py` is safe **only because `worker.py`
+> gates it first**. Invoked directly, or from any second caller, it has no
+> protection. See Open item 2.
+
+**4. Neither file contains a path that writes a zero-byte object without
+raising.** Whatever produces them lives in the upload/CDN path or outside this
+repo. Fact 1 points the same way: the one thing the sleep delays is the PUT.
+
+## Two pieces of evidence that carry less weight than they appear to
+
+**The ~30 clean laptop runs prove almost nothing.** At a 1-in-40 rate, 30 runs
+come up clean 47% of the time *even if nothing was ever fixed*. The runs also
+used ids whose cache files were already fully written, so they never exercised
+the suspected races. Do not cite them as evidence of anything.
+
+**"It has not happened since" is not yet evidence.** It needs a denominator
+nobody recorded. Against a 1-in-40 baseline:
+
+| Consecutive clean staging uploads | Confidence the rate actually dropped |
+| --- | --- |
+| 119 | 95% |
+| 182 | 99% |
+
+Below roughly 119 the quiet period is ordinary noise. **Get the staging upload
+count since the sleep landed and write it here** — it is cheap and it decides
+whether there is still a bug to chase.
+
+## Leading hypothesis — untested
+
+The new runner image ships a different DNS resolver (via Priya; it has caused
+trouble for another team). Nobody has checked whether it touches thumbd.
+
+This is the only hypothesis currently consistent with all four established
+facts: it is environment-dependent (explains the laptop), it sits on the upload
+side (explains why a delay before the PUT and only before the PUT changes the
+outcome), and it can plausibly yield a torn connection or an empty body.
+
+Untested is not the same as likely. It is the first thing to check because it is
+unchecked and cheap, not because the evidence favours it.
+
+## What would settle it, highest value first
+
+1. **CDN-side record of the PUTs that produced zero-byte objects** — status code
+   and received content-length. This distinguishes "the body never arrived" from
+   "the object was created and never filled", and it probably ends the
+   investigation on its own. Nobody has looked at it.
+2. **Resolver behaviour on a runner**: time `socket.getaddrinfo` for the bucket
+   host in a *freshly started* process, repeatedly; watch for a slow or failing
+   first resolution per process. Diff `/etc/resolv.conf` and `nsswitch.conf`
+   against the previous runner image, and check whether the libc changed.
+3. **Ask the other team what their resolver symptom actually was.** If it was
+   slow first resolution per process, that matches a 2-second delay masking it
+   almost exactly.
+4. **Log, in `render.py`, the response status and `len(blob)` for every upload.**
+   The incident produced no per-upload record, which is why this is still open.
+
+## Open items
+
+1. **The sleep stays until the cause is known.** Removing it is the experiment,
+   not the cleanup. Run it only with the upload logging from item 4 in place and
+   an upload counter running — otherwise a recurrence is as uninterpretable as
+   the original incident was.
+2. **`render.py`'s unlocked read is a real defect, currently masked.** It is
+   deliberately left alone: changing it while the zero-byte cause is unknown
+   adds a second variable to any recurrence. Fix it in an isolated change once
+   this is closed — or immediately if a second caller of `render.py` appears,
+   since the masking disappears at that moment.
+
+## Dead ends — already tried, do not redo
+
+- Reproducing by running `render.py` directly on a dev machine.
+- Lock-ordering theories about `worker.py`. It locks correctly.
+- The theory that truncated cache reads yield empty thumbnails. Measured: they
+  raise.
